# IP Addressing
IP (Internet Protocol) Addressing is a method of giving a unique address (identity) to every device connected to a network. It helps devices find, identify, and communicate with each other.
> Why is IP Addressing Needed?
Without an IP address:
Devices cannot identify each other.
Data cannot reach the correct destination.
Internet communication becomes impossible.

Think of it like a home address.
Home Address → Helps deliver a parcel.
IP Address → Helps deliver data (packets).
>Real-Life Example
Suppose you send a WhatsApp message to your friend.
Flow:
Your Mobile
      ↓
Internet
      ↓
Friend's Mobile
How does the message reach the correct phone?
Because both phones have unique IP addresses.
Without IP addresses, the internet wouldn't know where to send the message.
>Types of IP Addresses
A) IPv4
32-bit address
Four numbers separated by dots
Most commonly used
Example:
192.168.10.5
Maximum addresses:
About 4.3 Billion
B) IPv6
128-bit address
Uses hexadecimal numbers
Created because IPv4 addresses are running out.
Example:
2001:db8::1
Maximum addresses:
Very large (almost unlimited) 
> Static IP vs Dynamic IP
A) Static IP
Never changes
Fixed address
Used for:
Servers
Company websites
CCTV
Email servers
Example:
203.10.15.25
B) Dynamic IP
Changes automatically
Assigned by DHCP
Used for:
Home Wi-Fi
Mobile phones
Laptops
> Structure of an IP Address
Example:
192.168.1.25
It has two parts:
192.168.1   → Network ID
25          → Host ID
Network ID → Identifies the network.
Host ID → Identifies the specific device.
> Where is IP Addressing Used?
IP addressing is used almost everywhere.
Examples:
Internet browsing
Email
Online banking
Cloud computing
Video calls
> Use Cases
Use Case 1: Office Network
Printer
      |
Laptop ---- Router ---- Internet
      |
Desktop
Each device gets a different IP address.

Example:
Printer  → 192.168.1.5
Laptop   → 192.168.1.10
Desktop  → 192.168.1.20
> Advantages of IP Addressing
Unique identification
Easy communication
Supports Internet access
Helps in routing data
> What is the difference between Public IP and Private IP?
"A Public IP is used on the Internet and is globally unique, while a Private IP is used within a local network such as a home or office and is not accessible directly from the Internet."
> Can two devices have the same IP address?
"On the same network, two devices cannot have the same IP address because it causes an IP conflict. However, devices on different private networks can use the same private IP addresses."
> Who assigns IP addresses?
"Public IP addresses are assigned by the Internet Service Provider (ISP). Private IP addresses are usually assigned automatically by a router using DHCP."

# IPv4
IPv4 (Internet Protocol Version 4) is the fourth version of the Internet Protocol. It is used to uniquely identify devices on a network and allows them to communicate with each other over the Internet.
> Why is IPv4 Used?
)IPv4 is used to:
Identify devices on a network.
Send and receive data between devices.
Connect computers, mobile phones, servers, and routers to the Internet.
)Without IPv4:
Devices cannot communicate.
Data cannot reach the correct destination.
> Structure of an IPv4 Address
An IPv4 address consists of 32 bits divided into 4 parts (octets).
Example:
192.168.1.10
Each part is called an octet because it contains 8 bits.

192    .168    .1    .10
 ↑       ↑      ↑      ↑
Octet  Octet  Octet  Octet

Total:
8 + 8 + 8 + 8 = 32 bits
> Range of Each Octet
Each octet can have a value from:
0 to 255
Examples:
✅ Valid
10.0.0.1
172.16.1.20
192.168.1.100
8.8.8.8
❌ Invalid
300.1.1.1
192.168.500.1
Because an octet cannot be greater than 255.
> Parts of an IPv4 Address
Example:
192.168.1.25
It has two parts:
192.168.1   → Network ID
25          → Host ID
Network ID → Identifies the network.
Host ID → Identifies the specific device.
>Example
Imagine an office network.
Laptop  → 192.168.1.10
Desktop → 192.168.1.20
Printer → 192.168.1.30
All devices belong to the same network:
192.168.1
But every device has a different Host ID.
> Where is IPv4 Used?
IPv4 is used in:
Internet browsing
Office networks
Home Wi-Fi
Cloud servers
Mobile devices
> Advantages of IPv4
Easy to understand
Widely supported
Fast communication
Compatible with almost all devices
Simple configuration
> Limitations of IPv4
Limited number of addresses (about 4.3 billion).
Address shortage due to the growing number of Internet-connected devices.
Less secure than IPv6 (security features are not built-in).
> Why is IPv4 called a 32-bit address?
"IPv4 uses 32 bits divided into four octets, with each octet containing 8 bits. Therefore, the total size is 32 bits."
> How many octets are there in IPv4?
"There are four octets in an IPv4 address, and each octet ranges from 0 to 255."

# IPv6
IPv6 (Internet Protocol Version 6) is the latest version of the Internet Protocol. It is designed to replace IPv4 because IPv4 does not have enough IP addresses for the growing number of Internet-connected devices.
> Why is IPv6 Needed?
IPv4 can provide about 4.3 billion IP addresses, but with billions of smartphones, laptops, IoT devices, and cloud servers, these addresses are not enough.
IPv6 was introduced to solve this problem by providing a much larger address space.
> Structure of an IPv6 Address
An IPv6 address is 128 bits long and is divided into 8 groups.
Example:
2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334
Each group contains 4 hexadecimal digits and groups are separated by colons (:).
2001 : 0db8 : 85a3 : 0000 : 0000 : 8a2e : 0370 : 7334
> Where is IPv6 Used?
IPv6 is used in:
Cloud computing
Data centers
Mobile networks (4G/5G)
Smart devices (IoT)
Large enterprise networks
Modern websites
> Advantages of IPv6
Huge number of IP addresses
Faster and more efficient routing
Better security support (IPsec)
No address shortage
> Limitations of IPv6
Older devices may not support IPv6.
Migration from IPv4 to IPv6 can be time-consuming.
> Why was IPv6 introduced?
"IPv6 was introduced because IPv4 has a limited number of addresses. IPv6 provides a much larger address space to support the growing number of Internet-connected devices."
> What is the size of an IPv6 address?
"An IPv6 address is 128 bits long."
> What separator is used in IPv6?
"IPv6 addresses use colons (:) to separate eight groups of hexadecimal numbers."
> Give an example of an IPv6 address.
"An example is 2001:db8::1."
> Can IPv4 and IPv6 work together?
"Yes. During the transition period, many networks use Dual Stack, where devices support both IPv4 and IPv6 simultaneously."
